# A workspace is a live compliance record. It never belongs in version control.
*.db
# WAL mode writes two sidecars beside the workspace, and the -wal file holds
# committed records that are not in the .db yet. Ignoring the .db alone would
# leak the very records it is there to keep out.
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.log

# An evidence pack is the workspace again, in another format: record.jsonl
# holds every party, payment and decision. Ignoring only *.db would let the
# export feature walk the same records straight past that rule -- which it did
# once, on 14 August 2026, until someone looked at what was tracked.
evidence-pack/
*-evidence-pack/
record.jsonl

# Secrets are read from the environment, never from a file. This is a backstop:
# a credential committed once stays in the history forever, even after deletion.
# .env.example is the one deliberate exception: it holds no real values, only
# the variable names and how to get them, and is meant to be tracked.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
settings.local.json

__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/

# Screenshots are a working aid, not a deliverable.
.shots/

# mcp/'s own virtualenv (see mcp/README.md). This is the one place a
# third-party package is actually installed, and a venv is a build output,
# not a deliverable -- mcp/requirements.txt is what a checkout needs to
# recreate it.
.venv/

# Claude Code's own scratch state -- isolated worktrees it creates for
# parallel agents, session bookkeeping. Not part of this project.
.claude/
